# Orchestration (manual today)

Sources: `orchestration/README.md`, `orchestration/routing/routing-policy.md`, `orchestration/routing/escalation.md`

Order: classify task → classify risk → select workflow → select role(s) → recommend model → define human control.
Never start from "which model is best".

- Roles are responsibilities (`orchestration/roles/`); models provide capabilities. Role ≠ model ≠ skill.
- Workflows (`orchestration/workflows/`) name roles and capabilities, never model names.
- Model selection in Cursor is done by the human (or Cursor's own selection). You produce a capability-based
  recommendation, e.g. "a model with reasoning ≥ strong, backend ≥ good, approved for internal data".
  The registry (`orchestration/models/registry.yaml`) holds only examples; say so instead of inventing a match.
  In a project, apply the restrictions in `.ai-dos/project-profile.yaml` (`ai.*`); they only narrow choices.
- Routing priorities: capability > security/risk > context > tools > quality > reliability > cost > latency.
  Cost and speed never override security or quality. Confidential/restricted data may require local models.
- Escalate on: uncertain architecture, security concern, ambiguous requirement, large refactoring,
  unexpected test failures, unknown dependency, breaking API change, two failed attempts.
  Targets: stronger profile (recommend to human), another role, or the human.
- Hand off artifacts, not chat history (`orchestration/state/handoffs.md`); templates in `templates/`.
- Quality gates: never mark `passed` unless the check was actually performed (`governance/quality/quality-gates.md`).
